\title{Dual map}
\taxon{Lemma}
\date{2022-10-01}
\author{JB00}
\import{macros}

\p{Let #{V, W} be vector spaces over #{F} and #{\alpha \in \mathcal{L}(V, W)}. Then the map}

##{\alpha^* \colon W^* \to V^*, \quad \varepsilon \mapsto \varepsilon \circ \alpha}

\p{is an element of #{\mathcal{L}(W^*, V^*)}. It is called the \em{dual map} of #{\alpha}.}

\proof{
\p{#{\varepsilon \circ \alpha \colon V \to F} is linear by linearity of #{\varepsilon} and #{\alpha}, so #{\varepsilon \circ \alpha \in V^*}.}

\p{#{\alpha^*} is linear: for #{\theta_1, \theta_2 \in W^*},}

##{\alpha^*(\theta_1 + \theta_2) = (\theta_1 + \theta_2) \circ \alpha = \theta_1 \circ \alpha + \theta_2 \circ \alpha = \alpha^*(\theta_1) + \alpha^*(\theta_2),}

\p{and similarly, for all #{\lambda \in F}, #{\alpha^*(\lambda\theta) = \lambda\alpha^*(\theta)}. So #{\alpha^*} is linear, #{\alpha^* \in \mathcal{L}(W^*, V^*)}.}
}
